WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.620
With Taskmaster set to step into the upcoming
Black Widow movie, it's time to dive into

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.770
a long history that's strange even by the
standards of the Marvel Universe.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:13.200
From a school for supervillains to the South
American village populated by Hitler clones,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.080
here's the truth behind the Taskmaster.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.260
The Taskmaster made his first appearance in
1980's Avengers #195, arriving on the last

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:26.040
page to set up his full appearance in the
next issue and kick off a long career of supervillain

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:26.540
exploits.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.580
Created by David Michelinie and George Pérez,
he has a super-power usually referred to as

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.020
"photographic reflexes."

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:37.080
That's a fancy way of saying that if he sees
someone doing something, he can replicate

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.300
that action exactly, and he keeps that skill
for the rest of his life.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:44.180
That would be a pretty impressive ability
in the real world, but this is a dude who

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.860
lives in the Marvel Universe.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.960
He can settle in with some footage of Spider-Man
or Black Panther, and hop off the couch a

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.370
few hours later with the ability to perfectly
replicate their movements.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.340
Of course, there's a limit to all this: photographic
reflexes are Taskmaster's only power.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.000
That means that while he can watch Captain
America throwing his shield and replicate

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.880
the movement, he doesn't have Cap's super-strength
behind it.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.150
Still, he's come away from his many battles
against the Avengers with some pretty impressive

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:09.860
abilities.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.840
While he's done his share of hands-on villainy,
the Taskmaster's true calling has always been

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:15.720
in education.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.720
Of course, his "school," the Taskmaster Academy,
is less of a learning institution and more

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.960
of a for-profit training center for henchmen.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.609
For a low price, Taskmaster teaches classes
of aspiring supervillains not only how to

00:01:27.609 --> 00:01:30.920
fight like superheroes, but, more importantly,
how to fight against them.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:35.600
While this mostly functions as a pretty fantastic
explanation for where all the masked members

00:01:35.619 --> 00:01:40.109
of Hydra and AIM get the confidence to try
to punch out Iron Man, it's worth noting that

00:01:40.109 --> 00:01:43.640
it's not just the bad guys who've taken advantage
of the Taskmaster's skills.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.900
He's also been responsible for training a
few of the good guys as well.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.640
As a supervillain mercenary, the Taskmaster
doesn't have a whole lot of friends, but one

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.479
of his more enduring acquaintances came in
the form of another mercenary: Deadpool.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.799
The two characters had a rocky start to their
relationship, first meeting when Taskmaster

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:04.509
attempted to use Deadpool as a training dummy
for his students, only to be foiled when Deadpool

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:07.320
threw him off by breaking into a dance-off
during their fight.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.800
It turns out he can only counter your moves
if he can predict them, and as you may already

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.220
know, Deadpool can be pretty unpredictable.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.620
After that, one of the Taskmaster's most prominent
roles in the comics came when he was a member

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:22.620
of Deadpool's supporting cast, and in more
recent years, the two mercenaries have had

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.820
a more back-and-forth frenemy relationship.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.920
They've occasionally come to blows while on
opposite sides of a conflict, but Deadpool

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.520
was also willing to help Taskmaster out by
going easy on him in a fight so the skull-faced

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.240
opportunist could still get paid.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.700
Here's where it gets weird.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.950
While he's mostly shown up as a villain or
a supporting character, the Taskmaster has

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.480
dabbled in being a protagonist in his own
right thanks to two self-titled miniseries.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.690
The first is a relatively straightforward
action adventure, but that second story is

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:52.120
full-on bananas.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.380
The main premise is that the Taskmaster, ironically
enough, has a memory problem.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.680
His brain is so loaded up with Shang Chi's
punches and Daredevil's backflips that his

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.600
muscle memory has overwritten other, less
important things, like his past.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.400
To solve that, he tries to track down his
own memories.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.549
That leads him through things like an encounter
with the Don of the Dead, a Latin-American

00:03:11.549 --> 00:03:14.500
drug dealer who turns out to be a white American
S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.000
agent who'd taken the "Don of the Dead" identity
after Taskmaster trained him.

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:23.340
That, however, pales in comparison to where
that series ends up: Wewelsburg II, a small

00:03:23.349 --> 00:03:27.670
town in the South American Andes where Nazi
scientists escaped to after World War II to

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:28.960
attempt to clone Hitler.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.400
The most successful result was the long-time
Marvel villain the Hate-Monger, who first

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:38.349
showed up back in 1963, but the scientists
wound up with a gigantic stockpile of cloned

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:42.890
Hitler-brains, which leaked the Fürher's
"biochemical essence" into the local water

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:43.380
supply.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:44.120
The result?

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:49.380
Every man, woman, and child in the town essentially
has Hitler's brain, leading to constant warfare

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.379
among themselves for control of the area.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:55.120
So, you know, the next time someone tries
to tell you how serious the Punisher is or

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.740
whatever, remind them that he lives in the
same world as Hitler Town.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:03.659
Beyond Wewelsburg II and the Don of the Dead,
the big reveals of the Taskmaster series are

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.980
the title character's origin and real name.

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:10.099
For years, the Taskmaster had been getting
his orders from a secret organization called

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:10.620
"the Org."

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.980
But as it turns out, the Org doesn't actually
exist.

00:04:13.989 --> 00:04:18.780
Instead, it's the wife that Taskmaster doesn't
remember having, Mercedes Merced, who was

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.000
also his partner back when he was a S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.260
agent named, wait for it, Tony Masters.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:27.480
As Nick Fury tells Steve Rogers, the Taskmaster
has been a good guy all along.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:29.150
He just doesn't remember it

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:33.110
As Masters, he was sent to infiltrate the
supervillain community for S.H.I.E.L.D., taking

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.020
the Taskmaster identity as his cover.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.340
Unfortunately, thanks to a flawed prototype
of the Super Soldier Serum that expanded his

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:43.660
powers, all of those skills he'd absorbed
overwrote the personal parts of his memory.

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:48.320
Mercedes has been minimizing the damage for
years by acting as Taskmaster's handler and

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.060
pointing him in the right directions.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:54.389
Of course, once he discovers all this, his
brain immediately erases it in favor of another

00:04:54.389 --> 00:04:58.430
new fighting style, putting a heartbroken
Mercedes right back into the role of working

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:01.000
with a husband who doesn't remember she exists.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:05.440
While it's doubtful that, say, the Don of
the Dead will show up, it's not difficult

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.440
to imagine how Taskmaster might fit into Black
Widow movie.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:12.620
Most of Natasha Romanoff's history has to
do with her training in the Red Room, a secret

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:16.480
Soviet program designed to create a series
of highly trained agents who are given the

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:17.920
codename of Black Widow.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:22.190
With the Taskmaster's history and his ability
to serve as the perfect teacher in virtually

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:26.759
any style of combat, it's easy to picture
him as one of Natasha's trainers…and maybe

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:30.080
even the person who's sent to clean things
up once she defects to S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:34.320
Sure, that's a pretty straightforward espionage
plot, but it's also one that could fit neatly

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.720
into the world of the MCU.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:40.120
Then again, given that we've seen Infinity
Stones, the Celestials, and a talking raccoon

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.979
in those movies, there's always a chance that
Taskmaster and Widow will end up fighting

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.260
a climactic battle in Wewelsburg II.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:50.280
Seeing Marvel characters beating up a couple
hundred Hitlers might just make Black Widow

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.920
the feel-good movie of the year.

